<Chapter id="levy" label="Put the levy in the rules" />

## Put the levy in the rules

Your hands were always going to tire. So replace the targeted game with a structural rule:
a wealth levy plus an equal social return, applied on its own clock.

First, see the tool alone — freeze the trading.

<Deferred load={() => import('$lib/widgets/tax/TaxOnlyDemo.svelte')} label="levy demonstration" reserve="28rem" />

<Chapter id="tax-against-trade" label="Tax against trade" />

## Tax against trade

Now both rules run at once. Fair trades create the fluctuations that can concentrate wealth.
At its own cadence, the levy collects the same percentage from every holder and returns the
pool equally. What the room looks like after a fixed run depends on both dials.

So measure it. Put stake on one axis and levy rate on the other. Every point is one finite
experiment under the same protocol; its color records the measured outcome. You fill in the
map.

<!--
  WIDGET: PhaseDiagram (legacy component name) — the reader
  runs live rooms (SandboxWorld, fresh dice, ~2.3 s each) with snap-to-grid stake/tax dials;
  each finished room paints its own cell with its measured tail-mean Gini. After three
  plays, "fill in the rest" completes the 10×13 grid live in-page (two fixed seeds per
  remaining cell, chunked under a frame budget; cached per session). White contour = chosen
  finite-run Gini 0.5 outcome. Dashed curve = FITTED τ ≈ c·β². Neither is phase evidence.
-->
<Deferred load={() => import('$lib/widgets/phase/PhaseDiagram.svelte')} label="outcome map" reserve="42rem" />

If you fill the map, it adds a chosen Gini 0.5 contour and a square-law fit. The contour is
not a border between two proven phases. The fit asks a narrower question: how does the levy
needed for the same measured outcome change as the stake changes?

## The sandbox

Everything you have seen was this one machine wearing different costumes. Here it is with
every dial exposed: people, stake, a levy — and the room itself answers to your finger:
tap anyone to tax them on the spot. Break it. Save it. See what it takes.

